    {"id": "python-datetime-aware", "language": "python", "title": "Python: aware and naive datetime objects",
     "url": "https://docs.python.org/3/library/datetime.html#aware-and-naive-objects",
     "topics": ["time_handling", "datetime", "timezones", "ordering"],
     "text": "A datetime is aware when it carries time zone information (a tzinfo with a UTC offset) and naive otherwise. Aware objects identify a specific instant and can be compared and subtracted across time zones; a naive object does not identify an instant, and its meaning is up to the program. Ordering comparisons and subtraction between naive and aware datetimes raise TypeError, and they are never equal. Convert local times to UTC before ordering or doing arithmetic, and use astimezone() to view an instant in another zone."},
    {"id": "python-decimal", "language": "python", "title": "Python: decimal fixed-point arithmetic",
     "url": "https://docs.python.org/3/library/decimal.html",
     "topics": ["numeric_precision", "money", "rounding", "decimals", "floating_point"],
     "text": "The decimal module represents decimal numbers exactly, so values such as 0.1 carry no binary floating-point error. Construct Decimal values from strings or integers rather than floats, because Decimal(0.1) preserves the float's binary approximation. quantize() rounds to a fixed exponent such as two places with an explicit rounding mode; the default context rounds with ROUND_HALF_EVEN, while ROUND_HALF_UP is the familiar commercial rule. Use integer minor units or Decimal for money."},
    {"id": "python-floating-point", "language": "python", "title": "Python tutorial: floating-point arithmetic",
     "url": "https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/floatingpoint.html",
     "topics": ["numeric_precision", "floating_point", "rounding"],
     "text": "Binary floating-point numbers cannot represent most decimal fractions exactly, so 0.1 + 0.2 is not exactly 0.3 and repeated additions accumulate error. Compare floats with a tolerance such as math.isclose() instead of ==. round() works on the binary value, so round(2.675, 2) gives 2.67. Use the decimal or fractions modules for exact decimal results; math.fsum() reduces error when summing many floats."},
    {"id": "python-json", "language": "python", "title": "Python: json encoder and decoder",
     "url": "https://docs.python.org/3/library/json.html",
     "topics": ["serialization", "json", "parsing", "runtime_exception"],
     "text": "json.dumps converts dict, list, str, int, float, bool and None; other objects such as set, datetime or Decimal raise TypeError unless they are converted first. JSON object keys are always strings, so integer dictionary keys become strings after a round trip. By default json.dumps writes NaN and Infinity, which are not valid JSON; allow_nan=False rejects them. json.loads raises json.JSONDecodeError, a subclass of ValueError, for malformed input."},
    {"id": "python-exceptions", "language": "python", "title": "Python: built-in exceptions",
     "url": "https://docs.python.org/3/library/exceptions.html",
     "topics": ["runtime_exception", "nullability", "boundary_condition", "exceptions", "stack_trace"],
     "text": "KeyError is raised when a mapping key is not found, IndexError when a sequence index is out of range, TypeError when an operation is applied to an object of an inappropriate type (for example subscripting None), AttributeError when an attribute reference fails, ValueError for an argument with the right type but an inappropriate value, and ZeroDivisionError when a divisor is zero. RecursionError means the maximum recursion depth was exceeded. The last line of a traceback names the exception, and the frames above it show the call path."},
    {"id": "python-with-statement", "language": "python", "title": "Python reference: the with statement",
     "url": "https://docs.python.org/3/reference/compound_stmts.html#the-with-statement",
     "topics": ["resource_leak", "race_condition", "deadlock", "locks", "cleanup"],
     "text": "The with statement wraps a block in a context manager: __enter__ runs before the block and __exit__ runs after it, even when the block raises an exception or returns early. Using with for locks, files and connections releases them on every path, which prevents leaked resources and locks that are never released. A resource acquired without with must be released in a finally clause."},
    {"id": "python-sqlalchemy-pooling", "language": "python", "title": "SQLAlchemy: connection pooling",
     "url": "https://docs.sqlalchemy.org/en/20/core/pooling.html",
     "topics": ["resource_leak", "configuration", "connection_pool", "database_transactions"],
     "text": "A connection pool keeps a bounded number of database connections for reuse. A connection checked out from the pool must be returned on every path, including errors, normally by closing it or leaving a with block. Leaked checkouts exhaust the pool; further requests wait up to pool_timeout and then fail. Size pool_size and max_overflow for the expected concurrency, and enable pool_pre_ping to discard stale connections."},
    {"id": "go-sync-mutex", "language": "go", "title": "Go: sync.Mutex",
     "url": "https://pkg.go.dev/sync#Mutex",
     "topics": ["race_condition", "concurrency", "locks", "deadlock"],
     "text": "A Mutex is a mutual exclusion lock, and its zero value is an unlocked mutex. Lock blocks until the mutex is available and Unlock releases it. A Mutex must not be copied after first use, so pass structs that contain one by pointer. Pair Lock with a deferred Unlock so every return path releases the lock, and keep the check and the update of shared state inside the same critical section."},
    {"id": "go-time", "language": "go", "title": "Go: package time",
     "url": "https://pkg.go.dev/time",
     "topics": ["time_handling", "clocks", "timezones", "ordering"],
     "text": "A time.Time represents an instant with nanosecond precision and an associated Location. Compare instants with Equal, Before and After: == also compares the Location and the monotonic clock reading, so two equal instants can compare unequal. time.Parse uses the reference time Mon Jan 2 15:04:05 MST 2006 as its layout, and without a time zone indicator it returns a time in UTC. t.Sub(u) returns the elapsed Duration."},
    {"id": "go-encoding-json", "language": "go", "title": "Go: package encoding/json",
     "url": "https://pkg.go.dev/encoding/json",
     "topics": ["serialization", "json", "nullability", "type_assertions"],
     "text": "Unmarshal stores JSON numbers in an interface{} value as float64, objects as map[string]interface{} and arrays as []interface{}, so type assertions must use those types. Large integers lose precision as float64 unless the Decoder uses UseNumber. Marshal encodes a nil slice as null but an empty slice as []. Map keys are sorted when encoding, and only exported struct fields are marshalled."},
    {"id": "go-channels", "language": "go", "title": "Go specification: channel types",
     "url": "https://go.dev/ref/spec#Channel_types",
     "topics": ["deadlock", "concurrency", "channels"],
     "text": "An unbuffered channel communicates only when a sender and a receiver are both ready, and a buffered channel blocks senders while its buffer is full. Receiving from a closed channel returns the zero value immediately, and sending on a closed channel panics. When every goroutine is blocked, the Go runtime stops with 'all goroutines are asleep - deadlock!'. The sender should close a channel once no more values will be sent so that range loops over it terminate."},
    {"id": "java-concurrenthashmap", "language": "java", "title": "Java: ConcurrentHashMap",
     "url": "https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/17/docs/api/java.base/java/util/concurrent/ConcurrentHashMap.html",
     "topics": ["race_condition", "concurrency", "atomicity", "maps"],
     "text": "ConcurrentHashMap supports fully concurrent retrievals and highly concurrent updates, but a separate get followed by put is not atomic. Use putIfAbsent, computeIfAbsent, compute or merge to perform a check-and-update as one operation. Its iterators are weakly consistent: they never throw ConcurrentModificationException and may or may not reflect concurrent updates. Null keys and values are not allowed."},
    {"id": "java-bigdecimal", "language": "java", "title": "Java: BigDecimal",
     "url": "https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/17/docs/api/java.base/java/math/BigDecimal.html",
     "topics": ["numeric_precision", "money", "rounding", "bigdecimal"],
     "text": "BigDecimal represents an exact decimal value as an unscaled integer and a scale. divide without a rounding mode throws ArithmeticException when the exact quotient has a non-terminating decimal expansion, so pass a scale and RoundingMode. equals compares both value and scale, so 2.0 and 2.00 are not equal, while compareTo treats them as equal. Construct values from strings, because new BigDecimal(0.1) keeps the double's binary approximation."},
    {"id": "java-null-pointer", "language": "java", "title": "Java: NullPointerException",
     "url": "https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/17/docs/api/java.base/java/lang/NullPointerException.html",
     "topics": ["nullability", "runtime_exception", "autoboxing"],
     "text": "NullPointerException is thrown when null is used where an object is required: calling an instance method or accessing a field of null, or taking the length of a null array. Unboxing a null Integer or Long to a primitive also throws it. Recent Java versions describe which expression was null in the message. Check values that come from maps, optional JSON fields and wrapper types before dereferencing or unboxing them."},
    {"id": "java-concurrent-modification", "language": "java", "title": "Java: ConcurrentModificationException",
     "url": "https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/17/docs/api/java.base/java/util/ConcurrentModificationException.html",
     "topics": ["runtime_exception", "state_corruption", "iteration", "collections"],
     "text": "Fail-fast iterators of collections such as ArrayList and HashMap throw ConcurrentModificationException when the collection is structurally modified during iteration by anything other than the iterator itself, even within a single thread. Remove elements with Iterator.remove() or removeIf, or iterate over a copy. Fail-fast behaviour is a best-effort bug detector, not a guarantee."},
    {"id": "js-date", "language": "javascript", "title": "MDN: Date",
     "url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Date",
     "topics": ["time_handling", "timezones", "dates"],
     "text": "A Date stores one instant as milliseconds since the Unix epoch. Getters such as getHours() and getDate() use the runtime's local time zone, while getUTCHours() and toISOString() use UTC. Months are zero-based, so January is 0. Date-only ISO strings such as 2024-03-10 are parsed as UTC, but date-time strings without an offset are parsed as local time. Compare instants with getTime() rather than formatted strings."},
    {"id": "js-json-stringify", "language": "javascript", "title": "MDN: JSON.stringify()",
     "url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/JSON/stringify",
     "topics": ["serialization", "json", "bigint", "runtime_exception"],
     "text": "JSON.stringify omits undefined, functions and symbols in objects and turns them into null in arrays. It throws a TypeError for BigInt values and for circular references. NaN and Infinity become null. Map and Set have no enumerable own properties, so they serialize as {} unless they are converted first, for example with Object.fromEntries or Array.from."},
    {"id": "js-property-of-undefined", "language": "javascript", "title": "MDN: TypeError: can't access property of undefined",
     "url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Errors/Cant_access_property",
     "topics": ["nullability", "runtime_exception", "optional_fields"],
     "text": "Accessing a property of undefined or null throws a TypeError, which V8 reports as Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'x'). Find which expression is undefined: a missing object key, an out-of-range array element, or a function that returned nothing. Optional chaining (obj?.prop) returns undefined instead of throwing, but required fields should be validated explicitly."},
    {"id": "cpp-fundamental-types", "language": "cpp", "title": "C++ working draft: fundamental types",
     "url": "https://eel.is/c++draft/basic.fundamental",
     "topics": ["numeric_precision", "unsigned_integers", "overflow", "boundary_condition"],
     "text": "Unsigned integer arithmetic is performed modulo 2 to the power N, so subtracting a larger value from a smaller one wraps to a very large number instead of becoming negative. Signed integer overflow is undefined behaviour. Container sizes are unsigned, so an expression such as v.size() - 1 wraps when the container is empty; test for emptiness or convert to a signed type first."},
    {"id": "cpp-sequence-containers", "language": "cpp", "title": "C++ working draft: sequence container requirements",
     "url": "https://eel.is/c++draft/sequence.reqmts",
     "topics": ["boundary_condition", "runtime_exception", "indexing", "containers"],
     "text": "Sequence containers such as vector provide operator[] without bounds checking and at(), which throws std::out_of_range for an invalid position. Calling front() or back() on an empty container is not allowed. Inserting into or erasing from a vector can invalidate iterators, pointers and references to its elements, so do not keep using them across such operations."},
    {"id": "postgres-transaction-isolation", "language": "general", "title": "PostgreSQL: transaction isolation",
     "url": "https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/transaction-iso.html",
     "topics": ["database_transactions", "race_condition", "transactions", "isolation"],
     "text": "PostgreSQL's default isolation level is Read Committed: each statement sees data committed before it began, so two transactions can read the same row and then overwrite each other's update unless the write is made atomic. Repeatable Read and Serializable prevent more anomalies but can fail with serialization errors that the application must retry. A single conditional UPDATE, SELECT ... FOR UPDATE, or a unique constraint makes a check-and-write safe under concurrency."},
    {"id": "rfc9110-idempotent-methods", "language": "general", "title": "RFC 9110: idempotent methods",
     "url": "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-idempotent-methods",
     "topics": ["retry_idempotency", "idempotency", "retries", "http"],
     "text": "A request method is idempotent when multiple identical requests have the same intended effect on the server as a single request. PUT, DELETE and the safe methods are idempotent; POST is not. Clients may automatically retry idempotent requests after a connection failure, so a non-idempotent operation such as a payment needs an application-level mechanism, such as an idempotency key, before retries are safe."},
    {"id": "rfc9110-retry-after", "language": "general", "title": "RFC 9110: Retry-After",
     "url": "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-retry-after",
     "topics": ["retry_idempotency", "backoff", "retries", "configuration", "http"],
     "text": "The Retry-After response header tells a client how long to wait before a follow-up request, as an HTTP date or a number of seconds. Servers send it with 503 Service Unavailable, and RFC 6585 uses it with 429 Too Many Requests. Clients that honour it, and back off when it is absent, avoid amplifying an overload with immediate retries."},
    {"id": "rfc9111-caching", "language": "general", "title": "RFC 9111: HTTP caching",
     "url": "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9111.html",
     "topics": ["caching", "invalidation", "http"],
     "text": "A cache may reuse a stored response only while it is fresh, or after validating it with the origin server. Freshness comes from Cache-Control max-age or Expires; a stale response must be revalidated, for example with an ETag and If-None-Match. A successful unsafe request such as POST, PUT or DELETE invalidates stored responses for the target URI, which is the same rule an application cache needs after a write."},
    {"id": "rfc3339-timestamps", "language": "general", "title": "RFC 3339: date and time on the Internet",
     "url": "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3339",
     "topics": ["time_handling", "timestamps", "ordering", "serialization"],
     "text": "RFC 3339 timestamps such as 2024-03-10T09:30:00-05:00 include an explicit offset from UTC, and Z means UTC. Timestamp strings sort correctly as text only when they share the same offset and precision, so convert timestamps with different offsets to UTC before comparing or sorting them. A local time without an offset does not identify an instant."},
